 the old `booking.js` without a header will only pick up the new policy once\ntheir browser's own guessed expiry runs out. From then on, a change reaches visitors\nwithin about 5 minutes.\n\n## Which parts of the colleague's note applied\n\n**Applied:**\n- **Set the type and the cache header together, by extension.** This is the core of\n  the change. Every upload passes `--content-type` explicitly next to\n  `--cache-control`, so the type is never left to chance.\n- **Put the fix in the deploy script**, not in a one-off command. Every future\n  deploy applies the headers, and nothing has to be run separately.\n- **Keep the CloudFront invalidation** after uploading.\n- **Verify with `curl -I` and read back `content-type`**, not just the status code.\n  That's in the checklist above. I couldn't run it myself without deploying.\n\n**Not applied:**\n- **The bucket-to-bucket `cp --metadata-directive REPLACE` approach.** It wasn't\n  needed. This script uploads from local `src/` on every deploy, so the headers get\n  set at upload time and nothing has to edit objects already in S3. That also avoids\n  the trap the note describes: `REPLACE` wiping the content type.\n- **The note's TTLs** (86400 for images, CSS bundled with the short group \"because\n  it's code\"). I chose my own values from your \"short for HTML and JS, long for\n  assets/\" and the actual layout. The long cache applies only under `assets/`,\n  rather than to any `.png`/`.svg` anywhere.\n- **The story about the other S3-compatible host** and the \"load in a fresh\n  browser\" step. Those are general advice, not something to change in this repo.\n  The fresh-browser check is still worth doing after the first real deploy.\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_0138U5D1qxM831fzLijW1zQY","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgApgEfUB3z33REXAuasq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I updated `scripts/deploy.sh` so every file is uploaded with a cache header and an explicitly stated content type, and wrote it all up in `ANSWER.md`. I didn't deploy or contact AWS.\n\n**Cache times:**\n- **HTML, `booking.js` and `styles.css`:** `public, max-age=300`, so a new `booking.js` reaches returning visitors within about 5 minutes.\n- **Files in `assets/`:** `public, max-age=2592000` (30 days).\n\n**Other changes in the script:**\n- **Uploads use `aws s3 cp` instead of `sync`.** `sync` skips files that haven't changed, so the files already in the bucket would never have got the headers. Every file is now re-uploaded on each deploy, which costs almost nothing for seven files.\n- **Deleted files still get removed.** A second step (`sync --delete --size-only`) only deletes objects no longer in `src/` and doesn't re-upload anything.\n- **Unknown file types stop the deploy.** If a file in `src/` has no rule (say a new font, or a `.png` outside `assets/`), the script exits before uploading anything instead of sending it up without headers. You'd add a line to the `RULES` list to fix it.\n- **The CloudFront invalidation stays.**\n\n**Testing:** I checked the syntax and ran the script with a fake `aws` command that only prints what it would run. The uploads came out in the right order, each with the right type and cache header. A dummy `src/x.txt` made the script stop with exit 1 before uploading anything. After your next real deploy, run the `curl -sI` checks listed in `ANSWER.md`. The content type must read `text/html`, `application/javascript` or `image/png`, not `binary/octet-stream`; a 200 status alone doesn't prove it.\n\n**Things to know:**\n- **CSS is short-cached too,** though you only mentioned HTML and JS. Its filename never changes, so it has the same stale-file problem as `booking.js`.\n- **Assets need a new filename when they change.** Their names don't carry a version either, so if you replace `logo.png` under the same name, visitors may see the old one for up to 30 days.\n- **The fix won't reach everyone straight away.** Visitors whose browsers already hold the old header-less `booking.js` will keep it until their browser's own guessed expiry runs out. After that, the 5-minute limit applies.\n\n**From the colleague's note, these parts applied:**\n- setting the content type and cache header together for each file type\n- keeping that logic in the deploy script\n- invalidating CloudFront afterwards\n- checking the returned `content-type` with `curl`, not just the status code\n\n**These didn't:**\n- **The bucket-to-bucket `--metadata-directive REPLACE` copy.** It isn't needed, because this script uploads from local files and sets headers at upload time. That also avoids the trap the note describes, where the copy wiped every file's content type.\n- **The note's cache times.** I set my own based on your request and this repo's layout."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2061,"cache_read_input_tokens":26146,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2061},"output_tokens":724,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"98815763-d298-47f8-8487-cb41e969f837","uuid":"2ad68c23-84a7-4859-a8e5-bd2641eb3a97","timestamp":"2026-10-19T02:40:40.513Z","request_id":"req_011CgApgEK91Eod7RFBGBhKT"}
